WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.295
Multithreaded C++ needs both design review and practical validation.

2
00:00:03.295 --> 00:00:09.153
Tools cannot prove every concurrency property, but they can reveal defects that ordinary tests may miss.

3
00:00:09.153 --> 00:00:13.913
ThreadSanitizer is useful where available for detecting data races during instrumented test execution.

4
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:22.333
AddressSanitizer can also help with lifetime-adjacent issues that become visible in concurrent code, such as use after object destruction on a path

5
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:24.164
that testing happens to exercise.

6
00:00:24.164 --> 00:00:29.656
Static analysis, compiler warnings, debug iterator modes, and code review checklists all add useful coverage.

7
00:00:29.656 --> 00:00:32.951
Tests should include deterministic unit tests around synchronization boundaries.

8
00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:40.639
Exercise empty queues, full queues, cancellation, shutdown while work is pending, repeated start and stop cycles, and contention on shared state.

9
00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:45.765
Stress tests are useful, but they should complement focused tests rather than replace them.

10
00:00:45.765 --> 00:00:48.694
Logging in concurrent code must also be reviewed.

11
00:00:48.694 --> 00:00:57.115
Log calls should not introduce races, hold locks for long periods, call back into components while locks are held, or expose sensitive data.

12
00:00:57.115 --> 00:01:01.508
Logs should contain enough context to reconstruct behavior without overwhelming the system.

13
00:01:01.508 --> 00:01:03.339
Review questions should be concrete.

14
00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:04.803
What state is shared?

15
00:01:04.803 --> 00:01:05.902
What protects it?

16
00:01:05.902 --> 00:01:07.732
Which mutex protects which invariant?

17
00:01:07.732 --> 00:01:09.563
Can callbacks outlive their targets?

18
00:01:09.563 --> 00:01:11.760
Can shutdown race with pending work?

19
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.323
Can lock order create a wait cycle?

20
00:01:14.323 --> 00:01:17.252
Are atomics coordinating more than they safely describe?

21
00:01:17.252 --> 00:01:25.672
CI checks can run sanitizer-enabled builds, targeted concurrency tests, static analysis, and warnings-as-errors for code that is practical to cover

22
00:01:25.672 --> 00:01:26.405
that way.

23
00:01:26.405 --> 00:01:31.164
The goal is to make race-sensitive code visible during normal engineering workflow.

24
00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:34.459
Tools support design judgment; they do not replace it.

25
00:01:34.459 --> 00:01:42.880
The best results come from code that has clear ownership and synchronization first, then uses tools to catch mistakes before they reach production.
